First Author: Ding
Title: Discover important donor-recipient risk factors and interactions in heart transplant primary graft dysfunction with machine learning
Source number: 12

Very short summary: This retrospective UNOS study developed machine-learning models to predict primary graft dysfunction (PGD) after heart transplantation and to identify important donor-recipient risk factors and interactions. Five key findings were: (1) the cohort included 8008 heart transplant recipients, of whom 2146 (26.8%) had PGD; (2) the multilayer perceptron (MLP) performed best, with AUROC 0.868 and AUPRC 0.794; (3) 66 donor, recipient, operative, pair, and life-support features were used after filtering and feature engineering; (4) the top-ranked single feature was donor-recipient distance, while the top-ranked interaction was donor height with donor age; and (5) figures showed that higher recipient age, donor age, recipient BMI, donor BMI, and recipient diabetes increased PGD attribution, whereas higher recipient mean pulmonary artery pressure was associated with lower PGD attribution. Tables 1-4 and Figures 1-5 were used for this extraction.

