Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The cohort came from the national UNOS database and included 8008 heart transplant recipients, reducing single-center bias, although exclusions were applied for multiple transplants during the index hospitalization.  ￼
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Predictor domains were clearly described in Tables 1-2 and included recipient, donor, operative, pair, and life-support variables available around transplantation; no obvious post-outcome leakage is described in the retrieved text.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: The paper states that y indicates “the diagnosis of PGD after a heart transplant,” but the retrieved excerpt does not provide a full operational definition or ascertainment algorithm within the Methods.  ￼
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): Unclear | Quote/Reasoning: Strengths include multiple model comparisons, kNN imputation, 5-fold cross-validation, and explainability analyses, but uncertainty remains because hyperparameter tuning, calibration, and exact split sizes were not reported.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: This study directly addresses AI/ML for heart transplant PGD prediction using donor-recipient variables and is closely aligned with the review question.  ￼

